Chapter: CoapServer
  cites Encode chapter Coap
  cites Net chapter UdpIO
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 A CoAP server on a real socket, so that the device role an IoT product
 actually plays is exercised rather than assumed.

 `tools/coap-client.codex` proves Codex can ASK a CoAP server for
 something. This proves it can BE one, which is the more common shape:
 an LwM2M client is a CoAP server that a management peer pokes, and a
 sensor node answers rather than asks. It is driven by aiocoap's client,
 through `codex-vm -portfwd udp:<host>:5683`.

 IT ANSWERS ITS PEER, NOT ITS CONFIGURED PEER. A datagram carries no
 connection, so the sender's port arrives with the datagram
 (`ur-src-port`) and the answer goes back to it. codex-vm gives each host
 client its own synthetic gateway port, so replying to the port the
 request came from is what routes the answer to the right client.

 TWO ANSWERS, and the second matters as much as the first: a request for
 the resource it serves is answered 2.05 Content, and a request for
 anything else is answered 4.04 Not Found. A server that answered
 everything the same way would satisfy a test that only ever asked for
 something that exists.

Section: Configuration

  srv-local-mac : List Integer = [82, 84, 0, 18, 52, 86]
  srv-local-ip : List Integer = [10, 0, 2, 15]
  srv-gateway-ip : List Integer = [10, 0, 2, 2]
  srv-port : Integer = 5683

  srv-resource : Text = "codex"
  srv-payload : List Integer = [111, 110, 45, 100, 101, 118, 105, 99, 101]

  srv-poll-fuel : Integer = 4000000
  srv-requests : Integer = 2

Section: Reading The Request

 The Uri-Path is an option, so finding it is a walk over the option list
 rather than a field read. A request with no Uri-Path is a request for
 the root, which this server does not serve.

  srv-path-bytes : List CoapOption, Integer -> List Integer
  srv-path-bytes (opts) (i) =
    if i >= list-length opts then []
    else let o = list-at opts i
    in if o.number == coap-opt-uri-path then o.value
    else srv-path-bytes opts (i + 1)

  srv-wanted : List Integer = coap-text-to-bytes srv-resource

  srv-bytes-eq : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  srv-bytes-eq (a) (b) (i) =
    if list-length a /= list-length b then False
    else if i >= list-length a then True
    else if list-at a i /= list-at b i then False
    else srv-bytes-eq a b (i + 1)

Section: Answering

 The response is a piggybacked one: an Acknowledgement carrying the
 answer, matched to the request by message id and token. That is the
 common case in RFC 7252 and the one a constrained server should prefer,
 because it costs one datagram rather than three.

  srv-response : CoapMessage -> List Integer
  srv-response (m) =
    if srv-bytes-eq (srv-path-bytes (m.cm-options) 0) srv-wanted 0
    then coap-build-response CoapAcknowledgement coap-205-content (m.cm-msg-id) (m.cm-token) [] srv-payload
    else coap-build-response CoapAcknowledgement coap-404-not-found (m.cm-msg-id) (m.cm-token) [] []

Section: The Loop

 Bounded, and it says how many it will serve. A server that looped
 forever would have to be killed by the harness, and a killed process
 does not flush the output the harness reads.

  srv-serve : UdpSocket, Integer -> [Network.Read, Network.Write, Console.Write] Integer
  srv-serve (sk) (remaining) =
    if remaining <= 0 then 0
    else act
      r <- udp-io-recv sk srv-poll-fuel
      if r.ur-got == False then act
        (print-line-uni "coap-server: no request before the poll budget ran out")
        0
      end
      else when coap-parse (r.ur-data)
        is None -> act
          print-line-uni "coap-server: a datagram that is not CoAP"
          srv-serve sk (remaining - 1)
        end
        is Just (m) -> act
          sent <- udp-io-reply sk (r.ur-src-port) (srv-response m)
          print-line-uni ("coap-server: answered mid=" & show (m.cm-msg-id) & " path=" & show (list-length (srv-path-bytes (m.cm-options) 0)) & " bytes=" & show sent)
          rest <- srv-serve sk (remaining - 1)
          rest + 1
        end
    end

Section: Entry

  opening : [Network.Read, Network.Write, Console.Write] Nothing = act
    let sk = udp-io-open srv-local-mac srv-local-ip srv-port srv-gateway-ip srv-port
    in act
      print-line-uni ("coap-server: listening on guest udp " & show srv-port)
      served <- srv-serve sk srv-requests
      print-line-uni ("coap-server: served " & show served)
    end
  end
